{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310633,"nonce":"eq7WrFpU8i7FNN3IxxwqssKZLjs=","title":"ROOT CAUSE — work dispatched to SpinPool WORKER THREADS allocates 16-byte objects that are never freed (~8x). Workaround NUE_FLASH_POOL=0 = ...","message":"**ROOT CAUSE: `if (c) v = \u003cconst\u003e` in the SpinPool tight-spin loop boxed the\nconstant every time — `haxe_box_int_ptr`, 48 bytes, never freed.** The line was\n`if (tightUs \u003e 500) tightUs = 500;` in my own (then-uncommitted) adaptive-spin\nchange to `workerLoop`. Every worker thread ran it every 256 spins, forever.\n\n**MY REGRESSION** — introduced earlier in the same session, and since haxe-std is\nread FROM DISK at compile time, it contaminated EVERY measurement in the whole\ninvestigation. It also explains every confusing result: `NUE_MATMUL=0` looked\n\"flat\" only because the pool is never dispatched (no spinning); more workers /\nhigher spin budget / more dispatches = more leak; Q6_K vs INT8 differed only by\ndispatch cadence. Nothing was ever wrong with the INT8 kernel, the tensors, the\ncall path, or ownership.\n\n**FIX** = ternary clamp into a FRESH local (never reassign a local to a const):\n```haxe\nvar gap:Int = gapC.load();\nvar tightUs:Int = (gap \u003c 20) ? 20 : ((gap \u003e 500) ? 500 : gap);\n```\nSame for the pre-existing `holdUs` clamps below it.\n\n**RESULT** (Qwen Q5_0 server, 2 requests x 808 tok, byte-identical output):\nbox calls 9,000,000+ -\u003e under 1M; RSS +738..+1083MB -\u003e **-124MB (flat)**,\nmatching the no-pool baseline (-150MB). 80.6 tok/s pooled.\n\n**THE TOOL THAT FOUND IT** (keep it): env-gated counter in `haxe_box_int_ptr`\n(`RAYZOR_BOX_TRACE=1`, `RAYZOR_BOX_TRACE_INTERVAL=N`) printing call count,\nper-wrapper attribution, and — decisively — the **BOXED VALUE** plus\nvalue-shape buckets. `value=500` on 5M consecutive calls named the exact source\nline in one run, after backtraces/CLIF/LLVM-IR/MIR had all failed (JIT frames\non worker threads carry no frame pointer). **When a leak is a boxed primitive,\nlog the VALUE — a constant identifies its source line instantly.**\n\n`runtime/src/type_system.rs::haxe_box_int_ptr` = `haxe_box_int()` + `Box::new` +\n**`Box::into_raw`** → 48 bytes/call (a 32B + 16B pair, matching the `heap` size\nclasses) with NO matching free anywhere. Proven by an env-gated counter added to\nthat function (`RAYZOR_BOX_TRACE=1`, `RAYZOR_BOX_TRACE_INTERVAL`, still\nUNCOMMITTED in type_system.rs + wrapper counters in haxe_array/anon_object/\nconcurrency): **9,000,000+ calls in ONE 100-token request**, and\n`via_array=0 via_anon=0 via_thread=0` → **the JIT calls the helper DIRECTLY**\n(no Rust wrapper). Every sample is on a pool worker thread; worker JIT frames\ncarry no frame pointer so a backtrace cannot name the caller.\n\n**IT SCALES WITH SPINNING, NOT WORK** (same request, same throughput):\n| Config | boxes | tok/s |\n|---|---|---|\n| `NUE_POOL_SPINS=100` | **\u003c1M** | 73.7 |\n| `NUE_POOL_SPINS=200000` | **\u003e=5,000,000** | 72.5 |\n| `NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=2` | \u003c1M | 74.8 |\n| `NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=8` | \u003e=3,000,000 | 75.1 |\n\n=\u003e **The SpinPool IDLE SPIN LOOP allocates a leaked box per iteration.**\n**MITIGATION WITH ZERO THROUGHPUT COST: `NUE_POOL_SPINS=100`.**\n\nSUSPECTED expression (NOT yet proven): the atomic accessors in the spin loop\n(`stateC.load()`, `shutC.load()`). `rayzor/Atomic.hx` declares\n`public function load():T` — a GENERIC return on an extern abstract — while\n`runtime_mapping.rs:3555` maps it `=\u003e I32`. The erased-generic-return path is\nexactly what boxes primitives to `DynamicValue*`. An isolated 5M-load repro did\nNOT reproduce the boxing (and separately returned a WRONG sum — 119658496 vs the\ncontrol's 35000000 — worth investigating on its own), so treat the Atomic theory\nas unproven.\n\n`RAYZOR_DUMP_MIR` prints calls as `call fnN(...)` with PER-MODULE id tables.\nNeither \"reset at fn0\" nor \"nearest preceding decl\" resolves them correctly —\nboth produced confident, WRONG attributions (e.g. \"SpinPool boxes 26x in\nparallelRows\"). The tell: a 2-arg `call @haxe_box_int_ptr($line, $col)` when the\nreal fn takes ONE arg — those 2-const calls are\n`rayzor_update_call_frame_location(line, column)`\n(runtime/src/native_stack_trace.rs:377), a per-statement debug hook. **Do not\ntrust MIR fnN→name resolution without checking the ARITY matches.**\n\nNot tensors, not the kernels, not INT8. **Any Haxe band executed on SpinPool\nworker threads allocates 16-byte objects that are never freed.** The identical\nclosure run serially on the calling thread does not. (The user called it:\n\"check if threads are holding on to allocated objects and not freeing them.\")\n\nOne-request deltas (Qwen Q5_0 server, 808 tok; output byte-identical = 4422 in\nevery arm, so correctness is unaffected):\n| Config | 16B objects | RAM | tok/s |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| both pools ON | +77.1M | 1177MB | 56.9 |\n| `NUE_FLASH_POOL=0` (flash serial, matmul pool on) | +10.5M | **160MB** | **81.6** |\n| both pools off (`NUE_MATMUL_WORKERS=1`) | +1.6M | 25MB | 57.7 |\n| flash pool 1 vs 0, all else equal | +84.6M vs +10.5M | 1291 vs 160MB | 74.3 vs 82.2 |\n\n**`NUE_FLASH_POOL=0` IS A STRICT WIN TODAY: 8x less memory AND ~43% faster.**\nPooled flash is a net LOSS — the allocation storm outweighs the parallelism, and\nworkers being SLOWER than serial is itself the tell.\n\nRate ~77k objects/token ≈ 3.2k per layer per token ≈ one 16-byte box per\n(head, cached-KV-position) in FlashDecode's `while (l \u003c cacheLen)` x `bph` x\n`group` loops (`nue/nue/transformer/FlashDecode.hx`, `bandOneShifted` /\n`bandOneSigned`, dispatched via `sp.parallelRows(numKvHeads, band)`). QUADRATIC\nin context length — why long generations explode.\n\n1. **Stale tier / baseline function pointer.** SpinPool stores the closure in\n   `_fn` (SpinPool.hx:292); workers call it via `stealLoop(f, w)` (:187). If\n   that pointer is the pre-promotion baseline entry, workers run an unoptimized\n   BOXING version while the main thread runs promoted LLVM — explains the boxing\n   AND workers being slower. Check RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS / tier logs.\n2. **Thread-local never-freed arena.** `runtime/src/arena.rs:118`\n   `thread_local! { STRING_ARENA }` is a bump allocator explicitly \"valid until\n   program exit\"; chunks release only when the THREAD dies, and pool workers\n   live for the whole process — matches \"not freed until the server drops\".\n\n---\nEarlier framing (superseded but the A/B data stands). Qwen2.5-0.5B **Q5_0**\n(→INT8 QTensors at load) climbs\nunbounded on the server rig; **Q6_K is FLAT (855M) and fast (user-measured\n117.64 tok/s median)**. The 600MB-class baseline is intact — the regression is\nconfined to INT8 models.\n\n| Arm | Path | Footprint | tok/s |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `NUE_MATMUL=0` | Linear calls the extern DIRECTLY, bypassing Q4Matmul.matmul | **164M→805M FLAT** | **88-95** |\n| `NUE_MATMUL=1 NUE_HAXE_INT8=0 NUE_HAXE_Q8_0=0` | everything bails to Rust *through* Q4Matmul.matmul | 172M→2.7G | 53 |\n| `NUE_MATMUL=1 NUE_HAXE_INT8=1` | pure-Haxe INT8 kernel | 168M→3.0G | 43 |\n\n**The leak follows the CALL PATH, not the kernel** — the identical Rust kernel is\nflat from Linear and leaks through `Q4Matmul.matmul`. So it is NOT the Haxe INT8\nkernel. **Workaround for INT8 models today: `NUE_MATMUL=0`** (also the fastest).\n\n- Per-element Int→Float boxing. Every fold leaks at a different rate\n  (`Float*Int` 5.7G, `Float+=Float*Int` 5.7G, `SIMD4f.make` 2.5G, scalar\n  `var f:Float=dot` 4.1G) — a real per-element box would not vary like that.\n- Loop-carried SIMD4i32 / SIMD4f accumulators, closures, and\n  `SpinPool.parallelRows`: clean-room repro (45.6M elements, K=896) is **FLAT**\n  in all three modes (scalar / SIMD4f / SIMD4i32), 24-42MB, zero growth.\n- SIMD register pressure (1-row band still leaked 3.8G).\n- The activation quantiser (`NUE_INT8_NOQUANT=1` still leaks 5.2G).\n- Pool scratch, `Tensor.uninit` returns, wrapper dispatch (Q6_K uses all of\n  them and is flat).\n- Activation ownership: passing raw base instead of the Tensor + clone/free on\n  every XTQ bail → **no change** (2.7G/3.0G). Reverted.\n- Tail-return ARC (`return f()` vs `var y = f(); return y`) → no-op.\n\n`RZT_LEAK_STATS=1` (+ `RZT_LEAK_INTERVAL`) already exists in rayzor-tensors and\nprints `[leak-stats] allocs=N tensor_net_live=X rss=Y non_tensor~=Z` DURING the\nrun. **`tensor_net_live` pins at 1.3-1.4MB in every arm while RSS climbs\n500-800MB** — so no tensor data, no tensor handle, no pool entry leaks. The\ngrowth is entirely NON-TENSOR (Haxe/JIT-side heap). `heap` on the live process\nshowed the class: **~16-byte objects** (120M of them).\n\n**THE RIG THAT WORKS** (90s/arm, low variance, no poller): launch the server\ndirectly — `rayzor run llama-chat-server.rzb --native-lib \u003ctensors\u003e --native-lib\n\u003cnue_plugins\u003e --preset application --release --llvm -- --requests 2 --max 808\n--ctx 4096 --temp 0 --listen PORT`, wait for `[server] listening on`, drive it\nwith a raw-socket python client (send prompt, `shutdown(SHUT_WR)`, read to EOF),\nand diff first-vs-last `rss=` from `[leak-stats]`. Do NOT use bench_server.sh +\nvmmap polling for this — the poller steals CPU and inverts tok/s.\n\nQuantified (Qwen Q5_0, 2x808 tokens), tok/s from the SAME clean runs:\n- `NUE_MATMUL=0` (Linear→extern direct): **+95..130MB, 119 tok/s** ← flat AND fastest\n- `NUE_MATMUL=1` Haxe INT8 kernel: +738MB, 81 tok/s\n- `NUE_MATMUL=1` Rust kernel via wrapper: +579..782MB, 93 tok/s\n- Q6_K via wrapper: +236MB\n\n- Balanced ownership at the XTQ bails (clone+free, matching Linear.forward) —\n  looked like an 80% fix in one A/B (+1083→+212MB) but did NOT hold up: full\n  body with the fix still +579..782MB. That single promising number was run\n  variance, not signal.\n- \"Tensor-as-argument crossings leak, linear in count\" — plausible fit to 5 data\n  points, but eliminating ALL crossings (raw activation base into both Haxe\n  kernels + inlined bails) changed nothing (+782MB).\nRun-to-run variance on these arms is LARGE (direct +46..130, wrapper +579..782).\nNever conclude from one A/B pair; require the effect to exceed that spread.\n\nThe tensor side is instrumented and EXONERATED. Do the same for the **Haxe/JIT\nheap**: `runtime/src/profile.rs` has a `TrackingAllocator` with per-size\ncounters, but it is NOT installed as `#[global_allocator]` in the release\nbinary, which is why `[alloc-stats]` never prints there. Install it (or add a\nsize-bucketed live-count table) so the ~16-byte object class can be attributed\nto a site, then diff `NUE_MATMUL=0` vs `=1` on the clean rig above. Ten\nplausible-but-wrong hypotheses have died here; stop inferring from structure.\nSee [[feedback_benchmark_process_hygiene]] for the measurement traps.\n\nRelated: [[project_pure_haxe_tensor_ownership]],\n[[project_q5_0_int8_and_xtq_abi_fix]].\n\n`/usr/bin/time -l` peak RSS, llama-chat-server.rzb, 3 requests. **The regression\nis NOT from the 2026-08 session** — pre-session 18ee5121 = 1333 MB, mid\nc844629f = 1370 MB, HEAD cd488355 = 1232 MB (HEAD is the LIGHTEST).\n\nAdditive terms, each measured by A/B:\n\n| term | cost | note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `RZT_AMX_PREFILL=1` | **+207 MB** | 1486 vs 1279. The KNOWN-OPEN `cached_f16_weight_f32` leak |\n| Q5_K_M vs Q6_K (same ~490 MB file) | **+300 MB** | conversion copy retained |\n| ctx 512 -\u003e 4096 | **+149 MB** | 820/868/969 at 512/2048/4096 — legitimate, but scales |\n| floor (Q6_K, ctx=512, amx off) | **~338 MB** over file | JIT/LLVM/runtime |\n| `NUE_REQUANT_LM_HEAD=1` | +16 MB | negligible |\n| `NUE_HAXE_INT8=0` | **+113 MB** | INT8 SAVES memory; do not blame it |\n\nOverhead above file size: q5_k_m 800 MB, Q4_K_M 679 MB, Q6_K 500 MB.\n\n`static CACHE: Mutex\u003cOption\u003cHashMap\u003cusize,(usize,usize)\u003e\u003e\u003e` keyed by weight\nPOINTER, filled with `Box::leak(...)` and **never freed, never bounded** — its\nown doc says \"the f16 buffer is leaked\". This is the exact disease\n`cached_f16_weight` had before 13f29f4e deleted it in favour of ONE reusable\nscratch (a prefill touches each weight once, so caching buys nothing). Same fix\napplies. Was already recorded as open in\n[[perf_llamacpp_2026_08_mistral_prefill_gap]]; now quantified at +207 MB.\n\nQ5_K_M costs 300 MB more than Q6_K at the same file size, consistent with the\nsource Q5 weights staying alive after the INT8 conversion. Free the source.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_int8_model_footprint_climb` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}